  <footer class="provenance">
    <h2 class="prov-h">Sources, method and revisions</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <h6>Source of record</h6>
        <p>UN Department of Economic and Social Affairs, Population Division, World Population Prospects 2024: the standard CSV files of demographic indicators (medium and other variants) and population by five-year age group and sex, India (location 356). Data vintage <b id="prov-vintage" set:html={TX["prov-vintage"]}></b>. Urban, migrant-stock and refugee figures from the World Bank's World Development Indicators, vintage <b id="prov-wdi" set:html={TX["prov-wdi"]}></b>.</p>
        <p>Checked by hand: India's population on 1 July 2024 is 1,450,935,791 in both WPP 2024 and WDI; the gate checks the two sources agree every year.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>Method</h6>
        <p>Values are shown as published, converted from thousands for display. Broad-age shares and dependency ratios are computed here from the five-year age groups, and match WDI's own ratios. Nothing is interpolated or estimated by us.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>Revisions and breaks</h6>
        <p>Estimates and projections are separate instruments in separate files and are never joined. Each UN revision re-estimates the whole history. In WPP 2024's probabilistic-interval rows, life expectancy at 15, 65 and 80 and the age-specific mortality columns carry impossible values; those cells are not used.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>This guide</h6>
        <p>Version 1.0, built October 2026 from WPP 2024 and the World Bank's October 2026 WDI. Revised with each UN revision (every two years) and with each census.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="fine">
      <span>Measure of India, population</span>
      <span>Vintage <span id="prov-vintage2" set:html={TX["prov-vintage2"]}></span></span>
      <span>Set in Newsreader and Libre Franklin</span>
    </div>
  </footer>

